# Device-mapper devices the kernel made itself, from dm-mod.create= on the command line:
# the root's dm-verity device and those of the system extensions a VMM attaches
# (docs/sysext.md). lvm2's udev rules would name a dm device by asking dmsetup, and this
# image has neither — nothing in it creates dm devices from userspace. Without a DM_NAME,
# systemd's 99-systemd.rules sets SYSTEMD_READY=0 on every dm "add" and a mount of one
# waits until it times out, so this takes the name from where the kernel already put it,
# sysfs, and gives the /dev/mapper link with it. That is all lvm2's rules would do for
# these devices; anything a userspace tool creates is not touched.
SUBSYSTEM=="block", KERNEL=="dm-*", ENV{DM_NAME}!="?*", ATTR{dm/name}=="?*", ENV{DM_NAME}="$attr{dm/name}", SYMLINK+="mapper/$env{DM_NAME}"
